# 5. Create New Test: JSON Import Studio

When the student clicks `Create New Test`, open a dedicated test creation screen.

The central feature is a large, comfortable JSON editor where the student can paste a complete question set.

Support both direct paste and loading a local `.json` file.

Provide:

- A large monospace editor.
- Syntax highlighting.
- Line numbers.
- Format JSON button.
- Clear editor button with confirmation.
- Validate Questions button.
- Live question count.
- Subject distribution summary.
- Error messages showing the exact question index and field.
- A preview of parsed questions.
- A final `Continue to Test Configuration` button.

The system must parse and validate the actual JSON. Never silently discard malformed questions or replace invalid content with sample data.

## 5.1 Recommended JSON format

Use a documented, structured schema. Support a single object containing metadata and a `questions` array.

Example:

```json
{
  "title": "JEE Advanced Practice Test 01",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "subtopic": "Centre of Mass",
      "question": "A particle of mass 2 kg moves with velocity 3 m/s. Calculate its momentum.",
      "type": "integer",
      "marks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": 1,
      "answer": 6,
      "timeEstimateSeconds": 120
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "subject": "Chemistry",
      "subtopic": "Chemical Bonding",
      "question": "Which of the following molecules are polar?",
      "type": "multiple",
      "options": [
        { "id": "A", "text": "BF3" },
        { "id": "B", "text": "NH3" },
        { "id": "C", "text": "H2O" },
        { "id": "D", "text": "CO2" }
      ],
      "answers": ["B", "C"],
      "marks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": 2,
      "timeEstimateSeconds": 180
    }
  ]
}
```

Support an optional test title, subject, subtopic, marks, negative marks, and time estimate.

The question types must be represented internally using a strict TypeScript discriminated union and validated with Zod.

Use these canonical type names:

- `mcq`
- `multiple`
- `integer`
- `matching`
- `paragraph`

Optionally support sensible aliases such as `multi_select` and `numerical` through a normalization layer.

## 5.2 MCQ questions

Schema:

```json
{
  "id": "q3",
  "subject": "Physics",
  "question": "Which quantity is conserved in an isolated system?",
  "type": "mcq",
  "options": [
    { "id": "A", "text": "Momentum" },
    { "id": "B", "text": "Kinetic energy" },
    { "id": "C", "text": "Potential energy" },
    { "id": "D", "text": "Temperature" }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
  "marks": 4,
  "negativeMarks": 1
}
```

Requirements:

- Display all options as selectable answer cards or radio buttons.
- Allow only one selected option.
- Persist the selection when navigating between questions.
- Support unanswered and marked-for-review states.
- Evaluate the selected option against the correct option ID.

## 5.3 Multiple-correct questions

Schema:

```json
{
  "id": "q4",
  "subject": "Mathematics",
  "question": "Select all correct statements.",
  "type": "multiple",
  "options": [
    { "id": "A", "text": "Statement A" },
    { "id": "B", "text": "Statement B" },
    { "id": "C", "text": "Statement C" },
    { "id": "D", "text": "Statement D" }
  ],
  "answers": ["A", "C"],
  "marks": 4,
  "negativeMarks": 2
}
```

Requirements:

- Allow multiple selections using checkboxes.
- Compare answer sets correctly.
- Support exact-match evaluation by default.
- Allow configurable partial marking only when explicitly specified by the test configuration.
- Make the scoring rule transparent before the test begins.

## 5.4 Integer or numerical questions

Schema:

```json
{
  "id": "q5",
  "subject": "Mathematics",
  "question": "Calculate the value of 7 × 8.",
  "type": "integer",
  "answer": 56,
  "marks": 4,
  "negativeMarks": 0
}
```

Requirements:

- Provide a numerical input field.
- Support negative integers, decimals when enabled, and scientific notation if configured.
- Do not accidentally treat an unanswered field as zero.
- Define numerical precision and tolerance explicitly for decimal answers.
- Provide a configurable answer tolerance if necessary.

Integer questions should default to exact numerical matching.

## 5.5 Matching questions

Schema:

```json
{
  "id": "q6",
  "subject": "Physics",
  "question": "Match each physical quantity with its SI unit.",
  "type": "matching",
  "columnA": [
    { "id": "A", "text": "Force" },
    { "id": "B", "text": "Work" },
    { "id": "C", "text": "Power" }
  ],
  "columnB": [
    { "id": "1", "text": "Watt" },
    { "id": "2", "text": "Joule" },
    { "id": "3", "text": "Newton" }
  ],
  "correctMatch": {
    "A": "3",
    "B": "2",
    "C": "1"
  },
  "marks": 4,
  "negativeMarks": 1
}
```

Requirements:

- Render two clear columns.
- Allow the student to select or connect an item in Column A with an item in Column B.
- Support one-to-one matching by default.
- Provide a usable alternative to drag-and-drop for keyboard and touch users.
- Persist partial matching answers.
- Validate the matching schema before test creation.

## 5.6 Paragraph or comprehension questions

Support a shared passage followed by several independently answerable subquestions.

Schema:

```json
{
  "id": "passage1",
  "subject": "Physics",
  "subtopic": "Mechanics",
  "type": "paragraph",
  "paragraph": "A body moves along a straight line with constant acceleration...",
  "parts": [
    {
      "id": "part1",
      "question": "Find the acceleration.",
      "type": "integer",
      "answer": 2,
      "marks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "part2",
      "question": "Which statement is correct?",
      "type": "mcq",
      "options": [
        { "id": "A", "text": "Statement A" },
        { "id": "B", "text": "Statement B" }
      ],
      "answer": "B",
      "marks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": 1
    }
  ]
}
```

Requirements:

- Render the paragraph once, separately from its subquestions.
- Support MCQ, multiple-correct, integer, and matching question types inside paragraph groups.
- Maintain independent answers, marking, review status, marks, and timing for each subquestion.
- Display paragraph groups coherently in the question palette.
- Count independently answerable subquestions correctly in question totals and analytics.
- Do not accidentally award marks for an entire passage when only one subquestion is correct.

## 5.7 JSON validation and security

Before allowing the student to continue:

- Validate the entire JSON structure.
- Check for missing required fields.
- Check duplicate IDs.
- Check that correct answers reference existing option IDs.
- Check that matching references are valid.
- Check that paragraph subquestions use supported types.
- Check that marks and durations are valid numbers.
- Display all relevant validation errors clearly.

Question content must be treated as plain text or sanitized Markdown. Do not execute HTML, scripts, or arbitrary code embedded in imported JSON.

Store the original imported JSON so the test can be edited or recreated later.

